1.text:
The two inbred parental strains have haplotypes that can be denoted AAAAA and BBBBB at the five SNP loci. All gametes produced by these strains are non-recombinant and therefore identical to one of the two parental haplotypes.

An F1 individual therefore carries one fully-A chromosome and one fully-B chromosome. Every gamete it produces experiences exactly one crossover. With four intervals lying between the five SNPs, this yields precisely eight possible haplotypes, each containing exactly one transition (2 choices of which parental haplotype supplies the prefix, times 4 choices of interval).

An F2 individual therefore carries a pair of such single-transition haplotypes. Every gamete it produces again experiences exactly one crossover, now between the two F2 chromosomes. The resulting haplotype is formed by taking a prefix of one chromosome and a suffix of the other. Depending on the locations of the pre-existing transitions and the new crossover point, the product haplotype may contain 0, 1, 2 or 3 transitions:

- 0 transitions: the two pure parental strings (2 strings);
- 1 transition: 2 choices of starting allele \(\times\) 4 placements = 8 strings;
- 2 transitions: 2 choices of starting allele \(\times\) C(4,2) placements = 12 strings;
- 3 transitions: 2 choices of starting allele \(\times\) C(4,3) placements = 8 strings.

No haplotype containing four transitions can be generated, because each meiosis contributes only a single crossover and the input chromosomes to the final meiosis already contain at most one transition each. Consequently the reachable set in the F3 generation comprises exactly the 30 strings listed above.

Thus the number of distinct sequences observable on the arbitrarily chosen positive strand is 30.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {30}

